/* Original teaching expansions, written for a refresher-to-CV learning path. */
(() => {
  const guides={};
  const c=(label,min,max,step,value)=>({label,min,max,step,value});
  const add=(id,title,paragraphs,example,controls,predict,takeaway,notation)=>{guides[id]={title,paragraphs,example,controls,predict,takeaway,notation};};
  add(1,'An image has values, a shape and an arrangement',[
    'Start with a photograph reduced to four grayscale values. Each entry is a stored number; its position tells you which part of the image it belongs to. Arithmetic changes the values, while the shape and indexing convention explain how to interpret them.',
    'A scalar is one value, a vector is an ordered collection, and a matrix arranges values in rows and columns. A colour image adds a channel axis; a batch adds an example axis. These are representations you must name before a model can use them correctly.'
  ],'Transform four pixels, then inspect clipping',[c('Pixel scale a',0,2,.05,.5)],'Will doubling the scale double the displayed mean when a pixel reaches 255?','Separate the mathematical operation, the array shape and the display range. This is the same discipline you will use for neural-network inputs.','Subscripts identify a location rather than a new operation. Here r is row and c is column; a and b apply to every entry.');
  add(2,'A pipeline is a sequence of functions',[
    'Imagine following one pixel through preprocessing: first scale it, then shift it. Each operation consumes the previous output. Function composition gives a precise language for this sequence, including an order that you can trace with one concrete number.',
    'The outside function acts last. A symbol such as g(f(x)) is therefore an instruction to compute f(x) and pass its result to g. Exchanging the order is a new pipeline, and it often produces different values even though both pipelines use the same two operations.'
  ],'Compare scale-then-shift with shift-then-scale',[c('Scale a',0,3,.1,2)],'For which scale will the two orders agree?','Preprocessing order is part of the model specification. Write intermediate values when a nested expression feels hard to follow.','The circle means composition. Read g circle f as “apply f, then apply g”; parentheses say which value is supplied as input.');
  add(3,'A loss turns several errors into one objective',[
    'A prediction error first needs a reference: prediction minus observed target. That signed residual tells you whether a model is too high or too low. Averaging signed residuals can conceal mistakes because opposite signs cancel.',
    'Squared-error loss removes this cancellation, makes large mistakes more influential and gives training a scalar to minimize. The sum symbol is a compact loop over observations; dividing by their count produces mean squared error. Always state the reduction and target units.'
  ],'One adjustable residual among three observations',[c('Residual r',-3,3,.1,-2)],'Will reversing the sign change the mean squared error?','A loss can become smaller through better predictions or simply through rescaling the target. Compare models under the same objective and units.','A hat marks a prediction, the subscript i identifies an observation, and the summation runs from i = 1 to n.');
  add(4,'Keep score, probability and loss separate',[
    'A binary classifier begins with a score: a real number expressing which side of its decision boundary an input lies on. Sigmoid converts that score into a class-1 probability. The score and probability are different quantities, so they have different allowed ranges and different meanings.',
    'Training then compares the prediction with an observed class. Negative log loss uses the probability assigned to that class, which may be p or its complement 1 − p. A model can be confident and wrong; the logarithm makes that mistake expensive. Confidence still needs calibration checks on appropriate data.'
  ],'Follow a score all the way to its target-class loss',[c('Score z',-6,6,.1,1.1),c('Observed class y',0,1,1,1)],'Keep the score fixed and change the observed class. Which quantities will change?','Trace z → sigmoid(z) → probability of the observed class → negative logarithm. Do not take the logarithm of the score or forget the target.','σ is the sigmoid function, e is the natural-exponential base, and ln is its inverse on positive inputs. A probability of 0.75 means three quarters of the probability mass, not a score of 0.75.');
  add(5,'A position says where; a displacement says how far to move',[
    'Coordinates make geometric statements numerical. A position is meaningful relative to an origin and axes. A displacement compares two positions measured in the same convention and records the change coordinate by coordinate.',
    'The resulting vector has direction as well as magnitude. Its norm compresses that information into a distance, so it cannot replace the displacement when you need to reconstruct an endpoint. Image coordinates may point downward vertically; the algebra works as long as you keep that choice consistent.'
  ],'Move an endpoint while keeping its vertical coordinate fixed',[c('Endpoint horizontal coordinate',0,8,.1,5)],'Can the displacement change while its vertical component stays constant?','Check what each coordinate represents before adding vectors. Feature vectors and spatial positions share notation but need not share a geometric meaning.','Δ means a change: final minus initial. The two coordinates are separate components; a norm is one nonnegative scalar.');
  add(6,'Projection keeps only what lies along a chosen direction',[
    'Suppose you can represent a vector using only one line. Projection selects the point on that line closest to the original vector. The missing part is a residual perpendicular to the line, rather than an arbitrary remainder.',
    'The dot product measures alignment, but its raw size depends on the length of the direction vector. Dividing by the direction’s squared length corrects that scaling. This geometric calculation becomes the central condition in least squares and later in principal-component reconstruction.'
  ],'Project onto the diagonal direction',[c('Vector horizontal coordinate',0,6,.1,3)],'When will the projection residual vanish?','A projection coefficient is a scalar; the projected point is that coefficient times a direction. Keep them distinct.','The transpose turns a column into a row so that uᵀx is a scalar. The denominator uᵀu is squared length, not length.');
  add(7,'A matrix tells each output how to combine the inputs',[
    'Read a matrix row as a recipe for one output coordinate. Each row takes a weighted sum of all input components, and the collection of rows produces the output vector. Matrix multiplication is therefore a map, not multiplication of matching entries.',
    'Dimensions check whether the map is even defined. Once that check passes, columns reveal what happens to basis directions, while rows reveal how outputs are assembled. Affine neural-network layers add a bias after this map; batched row-vector notation transposes the arrangement, so state your convention.'
  ],'Stretch and shear a two-coordinate input',[c('Horizontal scale a',0,4,.1,2)],'What information disappears when the horizontal scale becomes zero?','A layer’s numerical entries, input convention and output dimensions together define its behaviour.','For a column input, A is outputs-by-inputs and Ax is the output. In the batched equation XW, rows are examples, which changes how shapes are written.');
  add(8,'Rank counts what survives a map',[
    'A matrix may have many entries without preserving every input direction. Rank counts independent directions in its outputs. The null space contains input changes that the matrix sends to zero, so these changes cannot be recovered from the output.',
    'A nonzero null vector provides a concrete demonstration: add it to any input, and the mapped result stays unchanged. Rank-nullity balances preserved and lost dimensions using the number of input coordinates. A dimension-reducing feature map always has some lost directions.'
  ],'Find an invisible input direction',[c('Mixing coefficient a',-2,2,.1,1)],'Will changing the coefficient change the number of lost directions, or only their orientation?','Information loss is a property of the map. A clever inverse algorithm cannot recover a direction that every output erased.','ker(A), also called the null space, is a set of vectors. Its dimension is a count; rank is another count, and their sum uses the input dimension.');
  add(9,'Exact equations and best fits answer different questions',[
    'An exact system asks for an input that satisfies every observation simultaneously. If two equations demand different values for the same expression, no input can satisfy both. This is inconsistency, even if the coefficient matrix has a familiar shape.',
    'Least squares asks a different question: which attainable output is closest to the observations? It can produce a useful compromise for an inconsistent system. Uniqueness is a separate issue; if a null direction remains, several parameter vectors can produce the same best fitted output.'
  ],'Two measurements ask for the same unknown sum',[c('Second observed sum',0,3,.1,2)],'When the observations agree, do they determine both unknowns uniquely?','Check consistency, identifiable quantities and uniqueness separately before choosing a solver.','In Ax = b, x contains unknown parameters and b contains observed targets. A residual measures their mismatch after applying A.');
  add(10,'Least squares is projection into a model’s output space',[
    'A model cannot produce every possible observation vector. A constant model, for example, can only produce outputs whose entries are all equal. Fitting chooses the point in this small output space closest to the observed targets.',
    'At the best fit, the residual is perpendicular to every available model direction. Otherwise moving a little in one of those directions would still reduce error. The normal equations express that orthogonality; they do not imply that every individual residual is zero.'
  ],'Fit one constant to three targets',[c('Third target',0,6,.1,3)],'If one target increases by three, how much will the fitted constant move?','Inspect the residual pattern as well as the fitted parameters. A best fit can still be a poor model of the data.','Aᵀr = 0 means the residual is perpendicular to each column of A. A hat marks the selected fitted parameter.');
  add(11,'A solvable system can still be sensitive',[
    'Some maps preserve one direction strongly and another only weakly. Recovering an input then requires undoing a very small scaling, which can amplify noise and rounding. The condition number measures this imbalance in a stated norm.',
    'This sensitivity belongs to the mathematical problem, while the chosen solver can add further numerical difficulties. Explicit normal equations square the singular-value ratio for a full-column-rank matrix. QR or SVD gives a better computational route without pretending the original observations contain more information.'
  ],'Compare a map with its Gram matrix',[c('Smallest singular value',.1,2,.1,.5)],'What happens to sensitivity as the smallest singular value approaches zero?','A tiny residual does not guarantee accurate recovered parameters in a poorly conditioned problem.','κ₂ uses the 2-norm. σmax and σmin are nonnegative singular values; the ratio assumes the relevant minimum is nonzero.');
  add(12,'Regularization makes a preference explicit',[
    'Fitting only the training observations can encourage complicated or unstable parameter choices. A regularizer adds a cost for a chosen property, such as large weight magnitude. It changes the optimization objective rather than merely changing the way a solver computes it.',
    'The tradeoff coefficient controls how expensive that property is relative to prediction error. A larger penalty generally shrinks ridge weights but can increase training error. Its useful strength depends on the data and task, so select it with validation rather than final test results.'
  ],'Balance fitting against squared weight cost',[c('Penalty strength λ',0,5,.1,1)],'Will the training error necessarily decrease when the penalty gets stronger?','Better training fit and better performance on unseen data are separate outcomes.','The squared norm sums squared weights. λ is a nonnegative multiplier; its interpretation depends on the exact loss normalization.');
  add(13,'Some directions stay on the same line after a map',[
    'An eigenvector is a nonzero direction that a square matrix maps to a scalar multiple of itself. The direction’s line survives the transformation even when its length changes or its orientation reverses.',
    'Repeated application makes the eigenvalue especially useful: each application multiplies that direction again, producing powers of the eigenvalue. This reveals growth, contraction and oscillation. A general matrix need not have a complete real eigenvector basis; the diagonal example here is intentionally simple.'
  ],'Apply a diagonal map three times',[c('First eigenvalue λ',-3,3,.1,2)],'How will a negative eigenvalue behave after one, two and three applications?','Eigenvalues describe signed action on special directions. Singular values instead describe nonnegative stretching and apply to rectangular maps too.','v must be nonzero. Av = λv is an equation about a direction; changing the scale of v does not change its eigenvalue.');
  add(14,'SVD ranks directions by how strongly they are represented',[
    'Singular-value decomposition describes a matrix as a rotation or change of orthonormal coordinates, followed by nonnegative stretching, followed by another coordinate change. It works for rectangular matrices as well as square ones.',
    'Keeping only the strongest components gives a low-rank approximation. The sum of squared discarded singular values quantifies squared Frobenius reconstruction error. This is a mathematical measure of retained matrix structure; it does not decide which details carry a useful visual label.'
  ],'Discard the weaker component of a diagonal matrix',[c('Second singular value',0,4,.1,1)],'What fraction of squared matrix energy remains when both singular values are equal?','Choose approximation rank using the intended task and reconstruction behaviour, rather than a large energy percentage alone.','U and V contain orthonormal directions. Σ contains singular values; the superscript T is transpose, and the subscript k indicates truncation.');
  add(15,'PCA finds directions of variation after centering',[
    'A cloud of observations may vary strongly along one line and weakly across it. PCA centres each feature, then chooses orthogonal directions that capture the most sample variation. The mean must be kept if you later reconstruct the original observations.',
    'Covariance summarizes spread and joint variation. Its leading eigenvector gives the direction of largest projected variance; its eigenvalue measures that variance. In this perfectly collinear example, one component captures all variation. Real images usually need more than one component.'
  ],'Rotate a three-point data cloud',[c('Horizontal spread',0,3,.1,2)],'Can the principal direction change while the data remains one-dimensional?','Fit centering and PCA on training data, then apply those fitted transformations to held-out observations.','Rows of Xc are centred samples; C is a feature-by-feature covariance matrix. The direction must be unit length to interpret the eigenvalue as projected variance.');
  add(16,'Reconstruction separates retained and discarded information',[
    'A reduced representation only allows reconstructions inside its chosen subspace. Once a direction has been removed, adjusting the retained coefficients cannot reproduce that missing component. The best coefficients are given by orthogonal projection onto an orthonormal basis.',
    'Reconstruction error can be decomposed into error from a poor coefficient choice and error that is unavoidable under the subspace restriction. This explains what changing the rank can fix. It also explains why fitting preprocessing using held-out examples leaks information even when no labels were used.'
  ],'Reconstruct a two-dimensional point using one axis',[c('Retained coefficient z',0,6,.1,3)],'Can any horizontal coefficient recover the missing vertical component?','A faithful reconstruction and a useful predictive representation are different evaluation goals.','A tilde or hat marks a reconstruction here. A norm is length; its square is the sum of squared reconstruction residuals.');
  add(17,'A derivative is the best local slope',[
    'A function value tells you where the curve is. A derivative tells you how its output changes for a small input displacement. Begin with a finite difference between nearby points, then consider what happens as their separation shrinks.',
    'This limit produces the tangent slope, which gives a local linear prediction. The approximation gets less reliable farther from the chosen point, especially where the curve bends strongly. A zero derivative can describe a minimum, maximum or another stationary point; it is not a general claim that the function is constant.'
  ],'Compare a quadratic’s secant and tangent',[c('Point x₀',-3,3,.1,2)],'Will a small finite-difference slope exactly equal the derivative on a curved function?','Keep function value, derivative and finite-difference estimate as three separate quantities.','The prime means a derivative with respect to the stated input. h is an input displacement, and “approximately equal” signals a local approximation.');
  add(18,'A gradient collects coordinate sensitivities',[
    'With several inputs, a change can point in many directions. Each partial derivative varies one coordinate while holding the others fixed. The gradient collects those partials into a vector with the same input-coordinate structure.',
    'To predict change along a chosen unit direction, take its dot product with the gradient. Alignment gives strong ascent, opposition gives descent, and perpendicular movement has zero first-order change. These statements use the Euclidean notion of step length; changing the metric changes what steepest means.'
  ],'Evaluate sensitivity along a fixed unit direction',[c('Horizontal point x',-3,3,.1,1)],'Can a direction decrease the function even when one coordinate partial is positive?','A gradient must be evaluated at a specific point. It gives local sensitivity, not the total change after a large move.','∇f is a vector of partial derivatives. The directional derivative is the scalar ∇fᵀu, with u normalized to unit length.');
  add(19,'The chain rule follows dependencies through a graph',[
    'Complex models are built from small operations. A computational graph names these intermediate values and records which outputs depend on which inputs. You can then differentiate each simple edge instead of expanding the entire final expression.',
    'Along a path, sensitivities multiply. When several paths reach a shared value, their contributions add. A Jacobian generalizes this rule to vector outputs by collecting derivatives in a matrix. Reverse-mode backpropagation applies these local rules efficiently for a scalar loss.'
  ],'Differentiate one affine prediction and its loss',[c('Weight w',0,4,.1,3)],'If the prediction exceeds its target, what sign should the weight gradient have here?','Draw the forward computation before differentiating. The graph and its shapes determine the chain-rule factors and transposes.','For a column input, a Jacobian has one row per output and one column per input. A backward scalar-loss gradient uses its transpose.');
  add(20,'Curvature explains the error in a tangent prediction',[
    'A first-order approximation uses the function value and gradient at a centre. A second-order approximation also uses curvature. In one dimension curvature is a second derivative; with several coordinates it becomes the Hessian matrix.',
    'The second-order term involves the displacement twice, so it becomes small quadratically as the displacement shrinks. For a quadratic objective the expansion is exact. For a general smooth function, the formula is an approximation with a remainder whose importance grows farther from the centre.'
  ],'Add the missing curvature term',[c('Displacement h',-1,1,.1,.5)],'If h is halved, how will the first-order error change for this quadratic?','Choose a small, stated neighbourhood before trusting a Taylor approximation or a finite-difference gradient check.','H is a matrix of second partial derivatives. In hᵀHh the displacement is a vector; the resulting curvature correction is a scalar.');
  add(21,'Reversing a conditional probability requires the base rate',[
    'A detector’s probability of a positive result given a real target is different from the probability of a real target given a positive result. The two condition on different populations, so one cannot simply be renamed as the other.',
    'Bayes’ rule builds the reversed conditional from joint probabilities. First count true and false routes into the observed positive group, then normalize by their total. The prevalence of the target changes that group’s composition, even if sensitivity and false-positive behaviour remain unchanged.'
  ],'Change prevalence while keeping detector behaviour fixed',[c('Target prevalence',.01,.9,.01,.2)],'Will a rarer target increase or decrease the probability that a positive result is correct?','Ask “among which group?” every time you read a conditional probability.','The vertical bar means “given”. P(A∩B) is a joint probability, while P(A|B) normalizes within the group where B occurred.');
  add(22,'Probability distributions describe outcomes; expectation summarizes them',[
    'A random variable turns an uncertain outcome into a numerical value. Its distribution lists probability mass for discrete outcomes or assigns a density across a continuous domain. A density height can exceed one because interval area, rather than height alone, is a probability.',
    'Expectation is a weighted average of possible values. It often falls between possible outcomes, and it is not a promise about the next observation. A sample mean averages what actually occurred; under appropriate sampling assumptions it estimates the population expectation.'
  ],'Inspect a Bernoulli distribution',[c('Probability of outcome 1',0,1,.05,.6)],'Must the expected value be one of the variable’s possible outcomes?','Distinguish a random variable, one realized value, a distribution and an estimator of its expectation.','E[X] means expectation, which weights values by their probabilities. For a continuous variable the weighted sum becomes an integral.');
  add(23,'Joint variation changes how errors combine',[
    'Variance measures spread around a mean using squared deviations. Covariance measures whether two centred quantities tend to have deviations of the same sign. Its value depends on their units; correlation divides out their marginal standard deviations.',
    'When quantities are added, covariance contributes to their combined variance. Positive association reinforces fluctuations and negative association can cancel them. Independence implies zero covariance when moments exist, but zero covariance by itself does not generally imply independence.'
  ],'Combine two unit-variance measurements',[c('Correlation ρ',-.9,.9,.1,.5)],'Can the variance of a sum be smaller than the variance of either individual measurement?','Measurement noise can be correlated across pixels and channels. Do not automatically treat every variance as additive.','Σ is a covariance matrix: diagonal entries are variances and off-diagonal entries are covariances. Standard deviation is the square root of variance.');
  add(24,'An estimator varies across samples too',[
    'Even a fixed population produces different finite samples, so a statistic computed from those samples has its own distribution. Standard error measures the spread of an estimator, such as a sample mean, across repeated sampling.',
    'Here independent normal observations with known standard deviation give a simple calculation: mean variance decreases with sample count, and standard error decreases with its square root. More observations improve precision under those assumptions, but they do not fix selection bias or create independence between adjacent frames.'
  ],'Change the number of independent observations',[c('Sample count n',5,100,1,25)],'How many times more observations are needed to halve standard error?','Every uncertainty calculation needs a sampling model. Bootstrap units and confidence-interval assumptions must match how the data was collected.','A bar denotes a sample average. SE describes an estimator’s sampling spread; it is distinct from the standard deviation of individual observations.');
  add(25,'Likelihood treats the data as fixed and the parameter as adjustable',[
    'Probability asks how likely possible data is under a specified parameter. Likelihood uses the same numerical expression but treats observed data as fixed while comparing candidate parameters. It does not become a normalized probability distribution over those candidates.',
    'Independent observations contribute a product of probabilities. Taking the logarithm turns that product into a sum and preserves its maximizer; negating gives a loss to minimize. This links probabilistic modelling to objectives that training algorithms can optimize.'
  ],'Fit one probability to three successes and one failure',[c('Candidate probability p',.05,.95,.05,.5)],'Why would a prediction of near certainty be bad when both outcomes have been observed?','State conditional-independence assumptions and whether your reported loss is a sum or mean.','The likelihood symbol is a function of parameters given fixed data. NLL means negative log likelihood, with the logarithm applied to observation probabilities.');
  add(26,'A prior contributes a modelling preference to the objective',[
    'Maximum likelihood uses only the observation model. Bayesian modelling also describes plausible parameters before seeing the current observations. The posterior combines these sources; a maximum a posteriori estimate selects its highest-density parameter rather than reporting the whole distribution.',
    'For a Gaussian observation model and zero-centred Gaussian weight prior, negative log posterior becomes squared data error plus a squared-weight penalty, up to constants and an overall factor. The variances determine the relative strength. This makes a familiar regularizer’s assumptions visible.'
  ],'Interpret ridge shrinkage as a Gaussian MAP estimate',[c('Prior precision λ',.25,4,.05,1)],'Will a tighter zero-centred prior move this estimate toward the observation or toward zero?','A MAP estimate is one point summary. It does not show posterior spread, and its numerical value depends on the stated prior and likelihood.','Precision is inverse variance. A prior with variance 1/λ becomes tighter as λ increases; constants independent of the parameter do not affect a MAP minimizer.');
  add(27,'Softmax probabilities are coupled across classes',[
    'A multiclass model produces one score per class. Softmax exponentiates these scores and divides by their sum, making a positive distribution across mutually exclusive classes. Increasing one score therefore changes more than one probability.',
    'Cross entropy compares that distribution with the target distribution. For a one-hot target, only the true-class negative log probability remains. The resulting score gradient is predicted probability minus target, a compact relationship that links uncertainty to the direction of learning.'
  ],'Change the cat score in a three-class prediction',[c('Cat score',-3,3,.1,1)],'If the cat score rises while the other scores stay fixed, what happens to their probabilities?','Use stable log-softmax calculations for extreme scores. Independent multilabel outcomes need a different model than this mutually exclusive softmax.','The sum in the denominator runs over classes, not examples. The class axis is part of the operation, especially for segmentation tensors.');
  add(28,'Correct labels and trustworthy probabilities are different properties',[
    'Accuracy checks a final class decision. Calibration checks whether probability statements agree with observed frequencies in comparable groups. A model can choose the right class often while giving confidence values that are systematically too high.',
    'Finite calibration groups contain sampling variation, so inspect counts and uncertainty rather than treating a frequency gap as exact population error. Keep model fitting, calibration choices and final assessment in separate data roles. A probability scoring rule also evaluates the full prediction rather than only its argmax.'
  ],'Score twenty equally confident binary predictions',[c('Predicted probability',.1,.9,.05,.8)],'Where will the constant prediction minimize Brier score for twelve positives out of twenty?','Metrics answer specific questions. Good discrimination, good calibration and low expected cost need not coincide.','A probability estimate is a model output. An empirical frequency is computed from outcomes in a group; their comparison requires a stated grouping and sample count.');
  add(29,'A local downhill direction still needs an appropriate step size',[
    'Gradient descent subtracts a multiple of the current gradient. The sign chooses local descent, while the learning rate chooses distance. These are separate decisions: a correct direction followed too far can overshoot and increase loss.',
    'A simple quadratic makes the mechanism visible. The distance to its minimum is multiplied by a factor after each update, so convergence depends on that factor’s magnitude. Real objectives have several directions with different curvature, which limits the learning rate that works safely across them.'
  ],'Take one step on a known quadratic',[c('Learning rate α',.05,1.5,.05,.25)],'Can a step in the negative-gradient direction finish with a larger loss?','Evaluate loss after updates and inspect gradients. “The learning rate is positive” is not a convergence guarantee.','The subscript t identifies the update number. α (some papers write η) is a chosen scalar rate, while ∇L is evaluated at the current parameters.');
  add(30,'Momentum remembers more than the latest batch',[
    'A mini-batch gradient is a noisy estimate of a larger-data objective’s gradient. Momentum combines the latest estimate with a state carried from earlier steps. This can sustain motion along persistent directions and smooth some oscillations.',
    'The exact state convention matters: some formulas accumulate gradients while others normalize an exponential average. The example uses a declared accumulated-gradient rule so every coefficient is visible. Historical state can oppose the current batch, which explains why momentum need not follow its latest gradient sign.'
  ],'Let an old velocity oppose a new gradient',[c('Momentum coefficient β',0,.95,.05,.8)],'When will the old and new contributions exactly cancel?','Record optimizer conventions, batch sampling and learning-rate schedules when comparing training runs.','v is the optimizer state in this example, β controls memory, and g is the latest batch gradient. These are not new model parameters.');
  add(31,'Curvature controls how much a gradient changes after a move',[
    'Convexity says that a local minimum is globally optimal within a convex domain. It does not guarantee that a particular iterative method reaches that minimum at any chosen learning rate. The shape of the objective determines how aggressive an update can be.',
    'A quadratic with adjustable positive curvature separates these ideas. Its minimizer remains zero while its slopes become steeper. A fixed rate can move from contraction to oscillation and then to divergence. Constraints add another issue: an unconstrained update may leave the permitted domain.'
  ],'Keep the rate fixed while changing curvature',[c('Curvature a',.5,6,.1,2)],'Will a fixed learning rate remain safe as the curve gets steeper?','State curvature or smoothness assumptions when using a convergence claim, and handle constraints explicitly.','The Hessian generalizes curvature to a matrix. Its eigenvalues describe curvature along directions; a positive-semidefinite Hessian supports convexity for smooth functions on a convex domain.');
  add(32,'Initialization sets the scale before learning begins',[
    'A layer sums many weighted inputs. If each weight keeps the same variance as the number of inputs grows, the preactivation spread can grow with the fan-in. Initialization rules choose a scale to keep activations and gradients useful at the start of training.',
    'The He rule accounts for the squared contribution lost through ReLU under simplifying independence and symmetry assumptions. Its preserved activation second moment is not the same as variance because ReLU has a nonzero mean. Initialization is a scale heuristic, so monitor actual activations and gradients rather than relying on its formula alone.'
  ],'Increase fan-in while keeping the predicted scale controlled',[c('Fan-in d',4,128,1,32)],'Should weight standard deviation increase or decrease as fan-in grows?','Check nonfinite values, saturation and vanishing or exploding gradient norms before attributing all training failures to model capacity.','Fan-in counts inputs to one unit. Variance uses squared units, standard deviation is its square root, and a second moment is E[X²] without subtracting the mean.');
  add(33,'Sampling discards what happened between observations',[
    'An image is sampled spatial data. High-frequency structure can produce the same stored samples as a different lower-frequency pattern, so it becomes impossible to recover the original signal uniquely from those samples alone. This ambiguity is aliasing.',
    'A twelve-sample cosine makes the ambiguity visible. Frequencies reflected around half the sampling rate share its samples. Before downsampling, low-pass filtering removes frequencies the new grid cannot represent. Coordinate alignment and interpolation conventions matter as well as the final array size.'
  ],'Sample a cosine at twelve fixed locations',[c('Signal frequency',1,11,1,4)],'Will frequencies four and eight give different sampled cosine values?','Resizing is a signal-processing operation. A sharper-looking resized image need not preserve the original information.','Frequency is cycles per unit distance or time. The sample index n is an integer; a continuous coordinate t can also take values between sample locations.');
  add(34,'A local filter is an aligned weighted sum',[
    'A filter response combines a local patch with a kernel. Multiply aligned entries and add their contributions to obtain one output. Sliding the kernel repeats this calculation at other positions, which makes the weights shared across the image.',
    'Mathematical convolution reverses the kernel before alignment; cross-correlation does not. Many deep-learning APIs call their unflipped learned operation convolution. Both are valid operations, but their conventions must match the formula, weights and any comparison with another implementation.'
  ],'Compare flipped and unflipped asymmetric kernels',[c('Middle kernel weight',-2,2,.1,0)],'Would a symmetric kernel reveal the difference between the two conventions?','Track the alignment and flip convention before interpreting a filter response as an edge or feature.','The summation indexes local offsets. Multiplication happens entry by entry inside the local sum; this is distinct from a matrix product across an entire image array.');
  add(35,'Output shape follows the positions a kernel can visit',[
    'A kernel must fit at each output position under a stated padding convention. Stride chooses the distance between consecutive kernel starts. Counting those starts gives the output length or height and width, so it is better than memorizing a shape formula without its geometry.',
    'Channels are a separate axis: a filter can sum over input channels while different filters produce output channels. Receptive field describes which input positions can influence an output. In deeper networks, spacing inherited from previous layers changes how a later kernel expands that field.'
  ],'Count starts for a seven-position input',[c('Stride S',1,3,1,1)],'Does a larger stride enlarge this first-layer kernel’s footprint, or only separate its starts?','Check both spatial alignment and tensor dimensions. Matching output sizes does not prove pixels refer to the same locations.','N is input length, K kernel length, P padding per side and S stride. The floor discards a final partial step; dilation would change the effective kernel size.');
  add(36,'Smoothing trades fine detail for reduced rapid variation',[
    'A normalized smoothing kernel combines neighbours using weights that sum to one. It preserves a constant signal away from boundary effects, but near an edge it mixes different sides and creates intermediate values. Smoothing therefore changes structure as well as noise.',
    'Fourier analysis describes the same filter by how it scales each frequency. The response at zero frequency captures constant preservation; its high-frequency response shows what happens to rapidly alternating patterns. Spatial and frequency descriptions are complementary views of one linear operation.'
  ],'Inspect one filter in spatial and frequency form',[c('Neighbour weight a',0,.25,.01,.2)],'Can a filter preserve average brightness while suppressing high-frequency detail?','Select smoothing for the noise and task, and state boundary conditions. Blurring can help sampling while hurting precise localization.','ω is angular frequency in radians per sample. H(ω) is a frequency response, not an image intensity or a probability.');
  add(37,'A nonlinearity changes which input regions behave differently',[
    'An affine layer combines inputs with weights and a bias. Stacking affine layers alone still produces one affine map, so extra depth would not create new nonlinear decision regions. An activation introduces an input-dependent response between those maps.',
    'ReLU keeps positive preactivations and suppresses negative ones. Its piecewise-linear regions make forward values and backward sensitivities easy to trace. The point where the pieces meet has no ordinary derivative, so an implementation uses a declared convention during differentiation.'
  ],'Move a ReLU’s activation boundary with its bias',[c('Bias b',-3,3,.1,0)],'What happens to the output sensitivity when the preactivation becomes negative?','Distinguish the affine preactivation from the post-activation feature. Each has a separate role in the computational graph.','W is a weight map, b is a bias, and the activation φ acts on the preactivation. “Affine” includes a shift; “linear” alone does not.');
  add(38,'Backpropagation reuses the forward computation',[
    'A two-layer network can be differentiated by tracing the same small operations used in the forward pass. Save values such as the preactivation, hidden activation and prediction. Their local derivatives then connect the scalar loss back to each parameter.',
    'Every factor has a source: the loss derivative, the output map, the activation derivative and the input map. A closed ReLU path can pass zero gradient even when the prediction is wrong. This is why a small gradient should be interpreted together with activation state and objective value.'
  ],'Trace a gradient through one hidden ReLU',[c('First-layer weight w',-2,3,.1,1)],'Can this example have a large loss but zero gradient through its first layer?','A gradient is a sensitivity of a stated loss to a stated parameter. Trace its path and account for every branch before applying an update.','The derivative factors multiply along a path. When a weight is shared by several examples or branches, contributions are summed or averaged according to the loss reduction.');
  add(39,'Training improvements address several distinct mechanisms',[
    'Regularization adds an explicit parameter preference to an objective. Normalization changes how intermediate values are scaled, while stable numerical formulas prevent avoidable overflow, underflow and cancellation. These mechanisms can interact, but one does not substitute for the others.',
    'Start by writing the exact regularized loss and differentiating it. A factor of one-half changes the coefficient that appears in its derivative. For simple SGD the squared-weight penalty resembles shrinkage; for adaptive methods, coupled penalties and decoupled weight decay can follow different updates.'
  ],'Combine a data gradient with a squared-weight penalty',[c('Penalty coefficient λ',0,1,.05,.2)],'Which part of the update changes when only the penalty coefficient changes?','Keep train/evaluation modes, loss normalization and optimizer conventions visible when reproducing a method.','The gradient of λ‖w‖²/2 is λw. A sum-loss convention versus a mean-loss convention changes the relative regularization scale.');
  add(40,'Autograd needs an independent check of the implemented objective',[
    'Automatic differentiation follows operation dependencies and applies local chain-rule rules. It computes derivatives of the implemented function, which may differ from the function you intended because of a wrong reduction, axis, detached value or graph construction.',
    'A finite-difference check changes one parameter slightly and measures the resulting loss change without using the analytic derivative. Central differences are useful near smooth points. Step size balances truncation and rounding errors, so use appropriate precision and avoid nondifferentiable kinks when diagnosing a mismatch.'
  ],'Check a cubic derivative with central differences',[c('Log₁₀ of difference step',-5,-.3,.1,-2)],'Will making the step arbitrarily tiny always make a numerical derivative more accurate?','Numerical agreement tests a local computation. It does not establish that the overall model, data protocol or objective is appropriate.','h is a finite parameter displacement. The subtraction uses two complete forward evaluations; the gradient returned by autograd comes from the recorded graph instead.');
  add(41,'An augmentation encodes an assumption about the target',[
    'A classifier’s output is a class decision derived from scores. Training-time transformations can encourage useful robustness by presenting varied inputs with the same target, but the shared label is an assumption about the task rather than a property of the image operation alone.',
    'A brightness change can preserve an object identity while changing an exposure-quality label. A flip can preserve an animal class but reverse a directional symbol. Inspect transformed examples and targets, then measure the intended effect against a controlled baseline instead of assuming more variation always improves performance.'
  ],'Change brightness while checking the label assumption',[c('Brightness scale',.2,2,.1,1)],'When clipping removes distinctions, is the same target still clearly supported by the image?','Inductive biases and augmentations should match the invariances your target genuinely has.','Argmax returns a class index, not the score or probability. An augmentation acts on inputs; its effect on targets must be specified separately.');
  add(42,'Detection adds geometry to the class decision',[
    'A detection prediction needs both a label and a spatial region. Box coordinates first require a convention: corner format, coordinate origin and whether endpoints are continuous or inclusive integer pixel positions. Inconsistent conventions change computed areas.',
    'Intersection over union compares shared area with the total area covered by either region. Subtract the intersection once when forming that union. IoU describes overlap, while a localization loss supplies a training objective; they are related but not interchangeable descriptions of every detection model.'
  ],'Slide a predicted box away from its target',[c('Horizontal box displacement',0,3,.1,1)],'How far can the box move before intersection becomes zero?','Compute geometry before applying an IoU threshold, and document the matching rules used in detection evaluation.','Vertical bars mean area here. The intersection symbol means overlap and the union means area covered by either box, counting common area only once.');
  add(43,'Background can make a failed segmenter look accurate',[
    'Segmentation predicts a class at each pixel. When an object occupies only a small part of the image, predicting background everywhere can achieve high pixel accuracy while recovering none of the object. A single overall accuracy therefore answers an incomplete question.',
    'Count recovered foreground, invented foreground and missed foreground explicitly. Foreground IoU and Dice use these counts without rewarding abundant true background. The training loss, probability threshold and final overlap metric need their own definitions, especially when using class weights or soft overlap objectives.'
  ],'Inspect a 100-pixel mask with ten foreground pixels',[c('Recovered foreground TP',0,10,1,6),c('False foreground FP',0,10,1,2)],'Can pixel accuracy remain high when foreground overlap is zero?','Evaluate the all-background baseline and report foreground behaviour, class averaging and empty-mask conventions.','TP and FN partition actual foreground; TP and FP partition predicted foreground. True negatives contribute to accuracy but not foreground IoU or Dice.');
  add(44,'Precision and recall condition on different groups',[
    'A score threshold selects which predictions count as positive. Precision asks what fraction of that selected set is correct. Recall asks what fraction of the actual positive targets has been recovered. Their denominators describe different groups, which is why neither can replace the other.',
    'Sweeping a threshold trades which mistakes are counted and produces a collection of precision-recall points. Detection adds one-to-one matching at a stated IoU threshold; average precision summarizes a declared convention over ranked detections. Segmentation evaluation may aggregate pixels or images, so its averaging rules also matter.'
  ],'Threshold six scored binary examples',[c('Positive decision threshold',0,1,.05,.5)],'Will precision remain defined if the threshold selects no examples?','Choose thresholds using the intended costs or a validation protocol, then report how positives are matched and metrics are aggregated.','Precision divides by predicted positives. Recall divides by actual positives. An undefined empty denominator needs an explicit reporting convention.');
  add(45,'The evaluation unit starts with the scientific question',[
    'A capstone should begin with a question that a metric and data protocol can actually answer. “Works on new videos” is different from “works on more frames from known videos”, so the intended generalization determines how samples must be grouped before splitting.',
    'Related images can share background, subject or near-duplicate content across a random frame split. A strong score then need not support the intended new-group claim. A meaningful simple baseline, a fixed test role and documented preprocessing make later comparisons interpretable.'
  ],'Compare group separation with independent frame assignment',[c('Held-out videos',4,10,1,4)],'How many independent groups does a hundred-frame dataset really contain in this example?','Write the claim and protocol before selecting an architecture. The split is part of what your result means.','An example is not necessarily an independent unit. Here a video is a group of related frames, so split counts should distinguish images from groups.');
  add(46,'A comparison must control more than the model name',[
    'A reported improvement can come from a changed split, extra training time, different preprocessing or a lucky seed. To study one design choice, keep those factors controlled and compare under a shared evaluation convention.',
    'Paired repeated runs show both effect and variation. Differences in percentages are measured in percentage points unless you explicitly compute a relative change. A small positive mean with inconsistent seed-level outcomes is weaker evidence than the headline alone suggests.'
  ],'Inspect paired differences across three illustrative runs',[c('Mean change in percentage points',-4,4,.1,1)],'Can the mean change be zero while individual runs differ?','Record dataset version, split, seeds, budget, objective and metric. A reproducible result includes the comparison’s conditions as well as its value.','Δ is new minus baseline in the selected metric. Its sign is beneficial only after you state whether higher or lower is better.');
  add(47,'An aggregate score depends on which cases you encounter',[
    'A model can perform differently across lighting, object size, source or population groups. The aggregate combines these results using the evaluation mixture, so a shift toward difficult cases can lower the headline score even if subgroup behaviour itself stays unchanged.',
    'Inspect representative failures and report subgroup counts, metrics and uncertainty. Different sample mixtures are one source of domain shift; changed conditional relationships and labels can create others. Use held-out evidence to diagnose the failure mechanism before claiming a proposed fix has solved it.'
  ],'Change the share of dark images in evaluation',[c('Dark-image share in percent',10,90,10,20)],'Can aggregate accuracy fall even when neither subgroup accuracy changes?','A limitation report should describe which conditions failed, how many examples support it and what remains uncertain.','Ptrain and Puse describe joint distributions over inputs and targets. A change in subgroup frequency can change an average without changing each conditional metric.');
  add(48,'Translate a method’s formula into testable operations',[
    'Reading a CV method means identifying the claim, definitions, assumptions and evidence around its equations. Start with a small numerical case where you can track every operation and dimension. A familiar symbol can hide a normalization or convention the implementation must reproduce.',
    'For example, a dot product equals cosine similarity only after the required normalization. You can derive this, inspect a non-unit example and test that the normalized score stays within its valid range. These local checks support mathematical consistency, while a separate controlled experiment supports the method’s predictive claim.'
  ],'Check a cosine-similarity claim using a non-unit vector',[c('Feature horizontal coordinate',-4,4,.1,3)],'Can the raw dot product exceed one while the normalized cosine score remains bounded?','Defend the connection from equation to code to experiment. State which assumptions were checked and which claims the evidence does not establish.','A norm in a denominator is an operation, not decoration. Normalization needs a nonzero vector, and its convention must match the method’s stated score.');
  window.CV_GUIDES=guides;
})();
